---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Simple from "../../layouts/Simple.astro";

const articles = (await getCollection("articles", ({ data }) => data.section === "pricing")).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order,
);
---
<Simple
  title="Analytics pricing"
  description="DataFast, PostHog, Amplitude, and Mixpanel pricing for founders, with the published steps taken from each vendor’s own pages."
  kicker="Pricing"
  lede="Some analytics products publish a monthly tier. Others publish a free allowance and then charge for volume. These guides show the steps that were actually on the page."
  crumbs={[{ href: "/", label: "Home" }, { label: "Pricing" }]}
>
  <p>DataFast, the analytics product at datafa.st, publishes monthly event tiers on its homepage. Starter starts at $9 and Growth at $19. The <a href="/pricing/datafast/">DataFast pricing</a> guide is that ladder, re-read on 25 November 2026. datafa.st/pricing returned 404, so the homepage is the source. The <a href="/reviews/datafast/">DataFast review</a> says what the product is, and which other companies share the name.</p>
  <p>PostHog, Mixpanel, and Amplitude publish a free event allowance and then charge for volume. The useful question is what the first paid step costs at your volume. Plausible and Fathom price marketing analytics differently, and GA4’s standard product is free. Those figures are also compared on the <a href="/alternatives/">alternatives hub</a> and the <a href="/compare/">compare hub</a>.</p>
  <p>DataFast amounts in the new guide were checked on 25 November 2026. The PostHog, Amplitude, and Mixpanel guides were checked on 24 November 2026 unless a page says a figure was re-read. Where a vendor only publishes “starts at $0” or a calculator, the guide says so and does not invent a rate.</p>
  <section slot="wide" class="band band-yellow">
    <div class="article-wide">
      <h2>Pricing guides</h2>
      <div class="guides">
        {articles.map((article) => (
          <a class="guide" href={`/${article.id}/`}>
            <span class="type">Pricing</span>
            <div>
              <h3>{article.data.title}</h3>
              <p>{article.data.description}</p>
            </div>
            <span class="meta">{`/${article.id}/`}</span>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Simple>
